---
title: Hotkeys Guide
id: hotkeys
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkey"},{"type":"text","value":" primitive is the primary way to register keyboard shortcuts in SolidJS applications. It wraps the singleton "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeyManager"},{"type":"text","value":" with automatic lifecycle management and reactive option support."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"logical-keys-and-physical-positions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Logical keys and physical positions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a logical binding when the shortcut should follow the character on the active layout. Use a physical binding when it should follow a keyboard position:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Binding"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Identity checked"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Mod+S"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ key: 'S', mod: true }"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Logical "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"event.key"},{"type":"text","value":", with conservative code fallback"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Mod+[KeyS]"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ code: 'KeyS', mod: true }"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exact "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"event.code"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Enter"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Logical Enter, including numpad Enter"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[Enter]"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[NumpadEnter]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Separate physical Enter positions"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every physical code uses brackets in strings, including names shared with logical keys such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[Enter]"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[F13]"},{"type":"text","value":". Supported codes are type-safe and available in autocomplete. Do not put a bracketed code in an object's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" field; use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"code"},{"type":"text","value":". A binding has either "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"code"},{"type":"text","value":", never both."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On a layout where the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"KeyQ"},{"type":"text","value":" position produces "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"A"},{"type":"text","value":" follows that character and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[KeyQ]"},{"type":"text","value":" follows the position. Logical ASCII letters remain layout-aware; conservative physical fallback helps with transformed output such as macOS Option keys. Exact matches take priority over weaker fallbacks among eligible registrations on the same target."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Callbacks expose the same distinction in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context.parsedHotkey"},{"type":"text","value":": check "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parsed.code !== undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" before reading its physical identity. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"formatForDisplay"},{"type":"text","value":" for labels; stored physical strings retain their brackets."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"basic-usage","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic usage"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nfunction App() {\n  createHotkey('Mod+S', () => {\n    saveDocument()\n  }, {\n    // override the default options here\n  })\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The callback receives the original "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"KeyboardEvent"},{"type":"text","value":" as the first argument and a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeyCallbackContext"},{"type":"text","value":" as the second:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkey('Mod+S', (event, context) => {\n  console.log(context.hotkey)       // 'Mod+S'\n  console.log(context.parsedHotkey) // { key: 'S', ctrl: false, shift: false, alt: false, meta: true, modifiers: ['Meta'] }\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can pass a hotkey as a string or as a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RawHotkey"},{"type":"text","value":" object (modifier booleans optional). Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mod"},{"type":"text","value":" for cross-platform shortcuts (Command on Mac, Control elsewhere):"}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())\ncreateHotkey({ key: 'S', mod: true }, () => save())           // Same as above\ncreateHotkey({ key: 'Escape' }, () => closeModal())\ncreateHotkey({ code: 'NumpadAdd', mod: true }, () => zoomIn())\ncreateHotkey({ key: 'S', ctrl: true, shift: true }, () => saveAs())\ncreateHotkey({ key: 'S', mod: true, shift: true }, () => saveAs())","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reactive-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reactive options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solid primitives accept property getters and accessor functions for reactive options. With an accessor, the hotkey updates automatically when dependencies change:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function Modal(props) {\n  createHotkey('Escape', () => props.onClose(), () => ({\n    enabled: props.isOpen,\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <Show when={props.isOpen}>\n      <div class=\"modal\">...</div>\n    </Show>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For scoped targets, use an accessor so the hotkey waits for the element to be attached:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function Editor() {\n  const [editorRef, setEditorRef] = createSignal<HTMLDivElement | null>(null)\n\n  createHotkey('Mod+S', save, () => ({ target: editorRef() }))\n\n  return <div ref={setEditorRef}>...</div>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"changing-a-binding","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Changing a binding"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass a new logical or physical binding through your framework's normal state mechanism. A recorder result such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Alt+[KeyS]"},{"type":"text","value":" can be passed directly to the same registration API. Keep an initial binding in application state if you want a reset button; the library does not need a separate preferences store."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"property-getters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Property getters"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Property getters and functions returning an options object are both supported. Read reactive state inside the getter. A plain value such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ enabled: currentValue }"},{"type":"text","value":" captures the value when that object is created."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The adapter reads option properties inside its reactive computation and updates registrations automatically. Callbacks such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onRecord"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onCancel"},{"type":"text","value":" remain functions; the adapter does not call them to resolve options. Tracking is shallow; callback bodies and nested objects are not evaluated to discover dependencies. Keep getters free of side effects. Ordinary option changes preserve registration identity. Changing the target moves the registration to that target."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nexport function SaveShortcut(props: { enabled: boolean; onSave: () => void }) {\n\tcreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => props.onSave(), {\n\t\tget enabled() {\n\t\t\treturn props.enabled\n\t\t},\n\t})\n\treturn null\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can also pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"() => ({ enabled: props.enabled })"},{"type":"text","value":". Both forms work for common options, sequences, and recorders. Property getters also work in per-definition options. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeysProvider"},{"type":"text","value":" follows replacement "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" objects and getters within defaults. Per-call options override provider defaults, and per-definition options override common options."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"default-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you register a hotkey without passing options, or when you omit specific options, the following defaults apply:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkey('Mod+S', callback, {\n  enabled: true,\n  preventDefault: true,\n  stopPropagation: true,\n  eventType: 'keydown',\n  requireReset: false,\n  ignoreInputs: undefined, // smart default: false for Mod+S, true for single keys\n  target: document,\n  platform: undefined, // auto-detected\n  conflictBehavior: 'warn',\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"why-these-defaults","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why these defaults?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most hotkey registrations are meant to override default browser behavior, such as using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Mod+S"},{"type":"text","value":" to save a document instead of showing the browser's \"Save Page\" dialog. So "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"preventDefault"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stopPropagation"},{"type":"text","value":" are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" by default, and you opt out per hotkey when you actually want the browser behavior."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"smart-input-handling-ignoreinputs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Smart input handling: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ignoreInputs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, hotkeys with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Ctrl"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Meta"},{"type":"text","value":" modifiers (like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Mod+S"},{"type":"text","value":") and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Escape"},{"type":"text","value":" key fire even when focus is inside input elements (such as text fields or text areas), and when focused on button-type inputs ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"type=\"button\""},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"submit\""},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"reset\""},{"type":"text","value":"). Shortcuts like save or close keep working wherever the user is focused. Single-key shortcuts, or those using only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Shift"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Alt"},{"type":"text","value":", are ignored within non-button inputs so they don't interfere with normal typing."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"hotkey-conflicts-conflictbehavior","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hotkey conflicts: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"conflictBehavior"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you attempt to register a hotkey that is already registered (possibly in another part of your app), the library logs a warning by default using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"conflictBehavior: 'warn'"},{"type":"text","value":" setting. That helps you catch accidental duplicate bindings during development, before they reach production."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"global-defaults-via-provider","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global defaults via provider"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can change the default options for all "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkey"},{"type":"text","value":" calls in your app by wrapping your component tree with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeysProvider"},{"type":"text","value":". Per-primitive options override the provider defaults."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { HotkeysProvider } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\n<HotkeysProvider\n  defaultOptions={{\n    hotkey: { preventDefault: false, ignoreInputs: false },\n  }}\n>\n  <App />\n</HotkeysProvider>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"hotkey-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hotkey options"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"enabled","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controls whether the hotkey is active. Defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":". Use an accessor for reactive control."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disabled hotkeys remain registered in the manager and stay visible in devtools; only execution is suppressed."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const [isEditing, setIsEditing] = createSignal(false)\n\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), () => ({ enabled: isEditing() }))","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"preventdefault","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"preventDefault"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatically calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"event.preventDefault()"},{"type":"text","value":" when the hotkey fires. Defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { preventDefault: false })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"stoppropagation","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stopPropagation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"event.stopPropagation()"},{"type":"text","value":" when the hotkey fires. Defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkey('Escape', () => closeModal())\ncreateHotkey('Escape', () => closeModal(), { stopPropagation: false })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"eventtype","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eventType"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whether to listen on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keydown"},{"type":"text","value":" (default) or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keyup"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkey('Shift', () => deactivateMode(), { eventType: 'keyup' })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"requirereset","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"requireReset"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":", the hotkey fires only once per key press. The key must be released and pressed again to fire again. Defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel(), { requireReset: true })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"ignoreinputs","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ignoreInputs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":", the hotkey doesn't fire when the user is focused on a text input, textarea, select, or contentEditable element. When unset, a smart default applies based on the hotkey type."}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkey('K', () => openSearch())  // Smart default: ignored in inputs\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())   // Smart default: fires in inputs\ncreateHotkey('Enter', () => submit(), { ignoreInputs: false })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"target","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"target"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The DOM element to attach the event listener to. Defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"document"},{"type":"text","value":". Can be a DOM element, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"document"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"window"},{"type":"text","value":", or from an accessor for reactive targets."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const [panelRef, setPanelRef] = createSignal<HTMLDivElement | null>(null)\n\ncreateHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel(), () => ({ target: panelRef() }))\n\nreturn <div ref={setPanelRef} tabIndex={0}>...</div>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When using an accessor for the target, the primitive waits for the element to be available before registering. Ensure the element is focusable (has "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tabIndex"},{"type":"text","value":") so it can receive keyboard events."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"conflictbehavior","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"conflictBehavior"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controls what happens when you register a hotkey that's already registered. Options: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'warn'"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'error'"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'replace'"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'allow'"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { conflictBehavior: 'replace' })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"platform","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"platform"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Override the auto-detected platform."}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { platform: 'mac' })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"automatic-dependency-tracking","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic dependency tracking"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solid's fine-grained reactivity means "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkey"},{"type":"text","value":" automatically tracks reactive dependencies. The callback always has access to the latest signal values:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function Counter() {\n  const [count, setCount] = createSignal(0)\n\n  createHotkey('Mod+Shift+C', () => {\n    console.log('Current count:', count())\n  })\n\n  return <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count()}</button>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"automatic-cleanup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic cleanup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The primitive automatically unregisters the hotkey when the component unmounts (when the owning reactive scope is disposed):"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function TemporaryPanel() {\n  createHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel())\n  return <div>Panel content</div>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"registering-multiple-hotkeys","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Registering multiple hotkeys"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you need to register several hotkeys at once, or a dynamic, variable-length list, use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkeys"},{"type":"text","value":" (plural) primitive:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createHotkeys } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nfunction Editor() {\n  createHotkeys([\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo() },\n    { hotkey: 'Escape', callback: () => close() },\n  ])\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"common-options-with-per-hotkey-overrides","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Common options with per-hotkey overrides"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass shared options as the second argument. Per-definition options override the common ones:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkeys(\n  [\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo(), options: { enabled: false } },\n  ],\n  { preventDefault: true },\n)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"dynamic-hotkey-lists","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic hotkey lists"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass an accessor for reactive arrays:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function MenuShortcuts(props) {\n  createHotkeys(\n    () => props.items.map((item) => ({\n      hotkey: item.shortcut,\n      callback: item.action,\n      options: { enabled: item.enabled },\n    })),\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The primitive tracks dependencies automatically and diffs registrations when the array changes."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"metadata-name-description-and-group","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Metadata (name, description, and group)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every hotkey registration can carry a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" object with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"description"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group"},{"type":"text","value":". Metadata never affects hotkey behavior, but it flows through to registrations and devtools, so you can build shortcut palettes and help screens from it."}]},{"type":"code","value":"createHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document' },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" option is typed as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeyMeta"},{"type":"text","value":", which ships with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"description"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group"},{"type":"text","value":" fields. You can extend it with additional properties using TypeScript declaration merging:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"declare module '@tanstack/hotkeys' {\n  interface HotkeyMeta {\n    icon?: string\n  }\n}\n\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document', icon: 'floppy', group: 'File' },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Group is descriptive metadata, not an execution scope. A shortcuts panel can group live registration views directly. Disabled registrations remain listed; unmounted registrations disappear."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"introspecting-registrations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Introspecting registrations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkeyRegistrations"},{"type":"text","value":" primitive to get a live view of all hotkey and sequence registrations. This is useful for building shortcut palettes, help dialogs, or devtools."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createHotkeyRegistrations, formatForDisplay } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nfunction ShortcutPalette() {\n  const registrations = createHotkeyRegistrations()\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h2>Keyboard Shortcuts</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <For each={registrations().hotkeys}>\n          {(reg) => (\n            <li>\n              <kbd>{formatForDisplay(reg.hotkey)}</kbd>\n              {reg.options.meta?.name && <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>}\n              {reg.options.meta?.description && <p>{reg.options.meta.description}</p>}\n            </li>\n          )}\n        </For>\n      </ul>\n      <Show when={registrations().sequences.length > 0}>\n        <h2>Sequences</h2>\n        <ul>\n          <For each={registrations().sequences}>\n            {(reg) => (\n              <li>\n                <kbd>{reg.sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')}</kbd>\n                {reg.options.meta?.name && <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>}\n              </li>\n            )}\n          </For>\n        </ul>\n      </Show>\n    </div>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The returned accessor yields an object with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hotkeys"},{"type":"text","value":" array and a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sequences"},{"type":"text","value":" array. Each hotkey entry carries the hotkey string, its options (including "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":"), and its enabled state; sequence entries have the same structure."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-hotkey-manager","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hotkey manager"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Under the hood, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createHotkey"},{"type":"text","value":" uses the singleton "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeyManager"},{"type":"text","value":". You can also access the manager directly if needed:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { getHotkeyManager } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nconst manager = getHotkeyManager()\nmanager.isRegistered('Mod+S')\nmanager.getRegistrationCount()","lang":"tsx"}],"headings":[{"id":"logical-keys-and-physical-positions","text":"Logical keys and physical positions","level":2},{"id":"basic-usage","text":"Basic usage","level":2},{"id":"reactive-options","text":"Reactive options","level":2},{"id":"changing-a-binding","text":"Changing a binding","level":3},{"id":"property-getters","text":"Property getters","level":2},{"id":"default-options","text":"Default options","level":2},{"id":"why-these-defaults","text":"Why these defaults?","level":3},{"id":"smart-input-handling-ignoreinputs","text":"Smart input handling: ignoreInputs","level":4},{"id":"hotkey-conflicts-conflictbehavior","text":"Hotkey conflicts: conflictBehavior","level":4},{"id":"global-defaults-via-provider","text":"Global defaults via provider","level":3},{"id":"hotkey-options","text":"Hotkey options","level":2},{"id":"enabled","text":"enabled","level":3},{"id":"preventdefault","text":"preventDefault","level":3},{"id":"stoppropagation","text":"stopPropagation","level":3},{"id":"eventtype","text":"eventType","level":3},{"id":"requirereset","text":"requireReset","level":3},{"id":"ignoreinputs","text":"ignoreInputs","level":3},{"id":"target","text":"target","level":3},{"id":"conflictbehavior","text":"conflictBehavior","level":3},{"id":"platform","text":"platform","level":3},{"id":"automatic-dependency-tracking","text":"Automatic dependency tracking","level":2},{"id":"automatic-cleanup","text":"Automatic cleanup","level":2},{"id":"registering-multiple-hotkeys","text":"Registering multiple hotkeys","level":2},{"id":"common-options-with-per-hotkey-overrides","text":"Common options with per-hotkey overrides","level":3},{"id":"dynamic-hotkey-lists","text":"Dynamic hotkey lists","level":3},{"id":"metadata-name-description-and-group","text":"Metadata (name, description, and group)","level":2},{"id":"introspecting-registrations","text":"Introspecting registrations","level":2},{"id":"the-hotkey-manager","text":"The hotkey manager","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nThe `createHotkey` primitive is the primary way to register keyboard shortcuts in SolidJS applications. It wraps the singleton `HotkeyManager` with automatic lifecycle management and reactive option support.\n\n## Logical keys and physical positions\n\nUse a logical binding when the shortcut should follow the character on the active layout. Use a physical binding when it should follow a keyboard position:\n\n| Binding | Identity checked |\n| --- | --- |\n| `Mod+S` or `{ key: 'S', mod: true }` | Logical `event.key`, with conservative code fallback |\n| `Mod+[KeyS]` or `{ code: 'KeyS', mod: true }` | Exact `event.code` |\n| `Enter` | Logical Enter, including numpad Enter |\n| `[Enter]` / `[NumpadEnter]` | Separate physical Enter positions |\n\nEvery physical code uses brackets in strings, including names shared with logical keys such as `[Enter]` and `[F13]`. Supported codes are type-safe and available in autocomplete. Do not put a bracketed code in an object's `key` field; use `code`. A binding has either `key` or `code`, never both.\n\nOn a layout where the `KeyQ` position produces `a`, `A` follows that character and `[KeyQ]` follows the position. Logical ASCII letters remain layout-aware; conservative physical fallback helps with transformed output such as macOS Option keys. Exact matches take priority over weaker fallbacks among eligible registrations on the same target.\n\nCallbacks expose the same distinction in `context.parsedHotkey`: check `parsed.code !== undefined` before reading its physical identity. Use `formatForDisplay` for labels; stored physical strings retain their brackets.\n\n## Basic usage\n\n```tsx\nimport { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nfunction App() {\n  createHotkey('Mod+S', () => {\n    saveDocument()\n  }, {\n    // override the default options here\n  })\n}\n```\n\nThe callback receives the original `KeyboardEvent` as the first argument and a `HotkeyCallbackContext` as the second:\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', (event, context) => {\n  console.log(context.hotkey)       // 'Mod+S'\n  console.log(context.parsedHotkey) // { key: 'S', ctrl: false, shift: false, alt: false, meta: true, modifiers: ['Meta'] }\n})\n```\n\nYou can pass a hotkey as a string or as a `RawHotkey` object (modifier booleans optional). Use `mod` for cross-platform shortcuts (Command on Mac, Control elsewhere):\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())\ncreateHotkey({ key: 'S', mod: true }, () => save())           // Same as above\ncreateHotkey({ key: 'Escape' }, () => closeModal())\ncreateHotkey({ code: 'NumpadAdd', mod: true }, () => zoomIn())\ncreateHotkey({ key: 'S', ctrl: true, shift: true }, () => saveAs())\ncreateHotkey({ key: 'S', mod: true, shift: true }, () => saveAs())\n```\n\n## Reactive options\n\nSolid primitives accept property getters and accessor functions for reactive options. With an accessor, the hotkey updates automatically when dependencies change:\n\n```tsx\nfunction Modal(props) {\n  createHotkey('Escape', () => props.onClose(), () => ({\n    enabled: props.isOpen,\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <Show when={props.isOpen}>\n      <div class=\"modal\">...</div>\n    </Show>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nFor scoped targets, use an accessor so the hotkey waits for the element to be attached:\n\n```tsx\nfunction Editor() {\n  const [editorRef, setEditorRef] = createSignal<HTMLDivElement | null>(null)\n\n  createHotkey('Mod+S', save, () => ({ target: editorRef() }))\n\n  return <div ref={setEditorRef}>...</div>\n}\n```\n\n### Changing a binding\n\nPass a new logical or physical binding through your framework's normal state mechanism. A recorder result such as `Alt+[KeyS]` can be passed directly to the same registration API. Keep an initial binding in application state if you want a reset button; the library does not need a separate preferences store.\n\n## Property getters\n\nProperty getters and functions returning an options object are both supported. Read reactive state inside the getter. A plain value such as `{ enabled: currentValue }` captures the value when that object is created.\n\nThe adapter reads option properties inside its reactive computation and updates registrations automatically. Callbacks such as `onRecord` and `onCancel` remain functions; the adapter does not call them to resolve options. Tracking is shallow; callback bodies and nested objects are not evaluated to discover dependencies. Keep getters free of side effects. Ordinary option changes preserve registration identity. Changing the target moves the registration to that target.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nexport function SaveShortcut(props: { enabled: boolean; onSave: () => void }) {\n\tcreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => props.onSave(), {\n\t\tget enabled() {\n\t\t\treturn props.enabled\n\t\t},\n\t})\n\treturn null\n}\n```\n\nYou can also pass `() => ({ enabled: props.enabled })`. Both forms work for common options, sequences, and recorders. Property getters also work in per-definition options. `HotkeysProvider` follows replacement `defaultOptions` objects and getters within defaults. Per-call options override provider defaults, and per-definition options override common options.\n\n## Default options\n\nWhen you register a hotkey without passing options, or when you omit specific options, the following defaults apply:\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', callback, {\n  enabled: true,\n  preventDefault: true,\n  stopPropagation: true,\n  eventType: 'keydown',\n  requireReset: false,\n  ignoreInputs: undefined, // smart default: false for Mod+S, true for single keys\n  target: document,\n  platform: undefined, // auto-detected\n  conflictBehavior: 'warn',\n})\n```\n\n### Why these defaults?\n\nMost hotkey registrations are meant to override default browser behavior, such as using `Mod+S` to save a document instead of showing the browser's \"Save Page\" dialog. So `preventDefault` and `stopPropagation` are `true` by default, and you opt out per hotkey when you actually want the browser behavior.\n\n#### Smart input handling: `ignoreInputs`\n\nBy default, hotkeys with `Ctrl`/`Meta` modifiers (like `Mod+S`) and the `Escape` key fire even when focus is inside input elements (such as text fields or text areas), and when focused on button-type inputs (`type=\"button\"`, `\"submit\"`, or `\"reset\"`). Shortcuts like save or close keep working wherever the user is focused. Single-key shortcuts, or those using only `Shift`/`Alt`, are ignored within non-button inputs so they don't interfere with normal typing.\n\n#### Hotkey conflicts: `conflictBehavior`\n\nWhen you attempt to register a hotkey that is already registered (possibly in another part of your app), the library logs a warning by default using the `conflictBehavior: 'warn'` setting. That helps you catch accidental duplicate bindings during development, before they reach production.\n\n### Global defaults via provider\n\nYou can change the default options for all `createHotkey` calls in your app by wrapping your component tree with `HotkeysProvider`. Per-primitive options override the provider defaults.\n\n```tsx\nimport { HotkeysProvider } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\n<HotkeysProvider\n  defaultOptions={{\n    hotkey: { preventDefault: false, ignoreInputs: false },\n  }}\n>\n  <App />\n</HotkeysProvider>\n```\n\n## Hotkey options\n\n### `enabled`\n\nControls whether the hotkey is active. Defaults to `true`. Use an accessor for reactive control.\n\nDisabled hotkeys remain registered in the manager and stay visible in devtools; only execution is suppressed.\n\n```tsx\nconst [isEditing, setIsEditing] = createSignal(false)\n\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), () => ({ enabled: isEditing() }))\n```\n\n### `preventDefault`\n\nAutomatically calls `event.preventDefault()` when the hotkey fires. Defaults to `true`.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { preventDefault: false })\n```\n\n### `stopPropagation`\n\nCalls `event.stopPropagation()` when the hotkey fires. Defaults to `true`.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Escape', () => closeModal())\ncreateHotkey('Escape', () => closeModal(), { stopPropagation: false })\n```\n\n### `eventType`\n\nWhether to listen on `keydown` (default) or `keyup`.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Shift', () => deactivateMode(), { eventType: 'keyup' })\n```\n\n### `requireReset`\n\nWhen `true`, the hotkey fires only once per key press. The key must be released and pressed again to fire again. Defaults to `false`.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel(), { requireReset: true })\n```\n\n### `ignoreInputs`\n\nWhen `true`, the hotkey doesn't fire when the user is focused on a text input, textarea, select, or contentEditable element. When unset, a smart default applies based on the hotkey type.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('K', () => openSearch())  // Smart default: ignored in inputs\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())   // Smart default: fires in inputs\ncreateHotkey('Enter', () => submit(), { ignoreInputs: false })\n```\n\n### `target`\n\nThe DOM element to attach the event listener to. Defaults to `document`. Can be a DOM element, `document`, `window`, or from an accessor for reactive targets.\n\n```tsx\nconst [panelRef, setPanelRef] = createSignal<HTMLDivElement | null>(null)\n\ncreateHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel(), () => ({ target: panelRef() }))\n\nreturn <div ref={setPanelRef} tabIndex={0}>...</div>\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> When using an accessor for the target, the primitive waits for the element to be available before registering. Ensure the element is focusable (has `tabIndex`) so it can receive keyboard events.\n\n### `conflictBehavior`\n\nControls what happens when you register a hotkey that's already registered. Options: `'warn'`, `'error'`, `'replace'`, `'allow'`.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { conflictBehavior: 'replace' })\n```\n\n### `platform`\n\nOverride the auto-detected platform.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { platform: 'mac' })\n```\n\n## Automatic dependency tracking\n\nSolid's fine-grained reactivity means `createHotkey` automatically tracks reactive dependencies. The callback always has access to the latest signal values:\n\n```tsx\nfunction Counter() {\n  const [count, setCount] = createSignal(0)\n\n  createHotkey('Mod+Shift+C', () => {\n    console.log('Current count:', count())\n  })\n\n  return <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count()}</button>\n}\n```\n\n## Automatic cleanup\n\nThe primitive automatically unregisters the hotkey when the component unmounts (when the owning reactive scope is disposed):\n\n```tsx\nfunction TemporaryPanel() {\n  createHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel())\n  return <div>Panel content</div>\n}\n```\n\n## Registering multiple hotkeys\n\nWhen you need to register several hotkeys at once, or a dynamic, variable-length list, use the `createHotkeys` (plural) primitive:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createHotkeys } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nfunction Editor() {\n  createHotkeys([\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo() },\n    { hotkey: 'Escape', callback: () => close() },\n  ])\n}\n```\n\n### Common options with per-hotkey overrides\n\nPass shared options as the second argument. Per-definition options override the common ones:\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeys(\n  [\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo(), options: { enabled: false } },\n  ],\n  { preventDefault: true },\n)\n```\n\n### Dynamic hotkey lists\n\nPass an accessor for reactive arrays:\n\n```tsx\nfunction MenuShortcuts(props) {\n  createHotkeys(\n    () => props.items.map((item) => ({\n      hotkey: item.shortcut,\n      callback: item.action,\n      options: { enabled: item.enabled },\n    })),\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe primitive tracks dependencies automatically and diffs registrations when the array changes.\n\n## Metadata (name, description, and group)\n\nEvery hotkey registration can carry a `meta` object with a `name`, `description`, and `group`. Metadata never affects hotkey behavior, but it flows through to registrations and devtools, so you can build shortcut palettes and help screens from it.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document' },\n})\n```\n\nThe `meta` option is typed as `HotkeyMeta`, which ships with `name`, `description`, and `group` fields. You can extend it with additional properties using TypeScript declaration merging:\n\n```tsx\ndeclare module '@tanstack/hotkeys' {\n  interface HotkeyMeta {\n    icon?: string\n  }\n}\n\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document', icon: 'floppy', group: 'File' },\n})\n```\n\nGroup is descriptive metadata, not an execution scope. A shortcuts panel can group live registration views directly. Disabled registrations remain listed; unmounted registrations disappear.\n\n## Introspecting registrations\n\nUse the `createHotkeyRegistrations` primitive to get a live view of all hotkey and sequence registrations. This is useful for building shortcut palettes, help dialogs, or devtools.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createHotkeyRegistrations, formatForDisplay } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nfunction ShortcutPalette() {\n  const registrations = createHotkeyRegistrations()\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h2>Keyboard Shortcuts</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <For each={registrations().hotkeys}>\n          {(reg) => (\n            <li>\n              <kbd>{formatForDisplay(reg.hotkey)}</kbd>\n              {reg.options.meta?.name && <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>}\n              {reg.options.meta?.description && <p>{reg.options.meta.description}</p>}\n            </li>\n          )}\n        </For>\n      </ul>\n      <Show when={registrations().sequences.length > 0}>\n        <h2>Sequences</h2>\n        <ul>\n          <For each={registrations().sequences}>\n            {(reg) => (\n              <li>\n                <kbd>{reg.sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')}</kbd>\n                {reg.options.meta?.name && <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>}\n              </li>\n            )}\n          </For>\n        </ul>\n      </Show>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe returned accessor yields an object with a `hotkeys` array and a `sequences` array. Each hotkey entry carries the hotkey string, its options (including `meta`), and its enabled state; sequence entries have the same structure.\n\n## The hotkey manager\n\nUnder the hood, `createHotkey` uses the singleton `HotkeyManager`. You can also access the manager directly if needed:\n\n```tsx\nimport { getHotkeyManager } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nconst manager = getHotkeyManager()\nmanager.isRegistered('Mod+S')\nmanager.getRegistrationCount()\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Hotkeys Guide\nid: hotkeys\n---\n\nThe `createHotkey` primitive is the primary way to register keyboard shortcuts in SolidJS applications. It wraps the singleton `HotkeyManager` with automatic lifecycle management and reactive option support.\n\n## Logical keys and physical positions\n\nUse a logical binding when the shortcut should follow the character on the active layout. Use a physical binding when it should follow a keyboard position:\n\n| Binding | Identity checked |\n| --- | --- |\n| `Mod+S` or `{ key: 'S', mod: true }` | Logical `event.key`, with conservative code fallback |\n| `Mod+[KeyS]` or `{ code: 'KeyS', mod: true }` | Exact `event.code` |\n| `Enter` | Logical Enter, including numpad Enter |\n| `[Enter]` / `[NumpadEnter]` | Separate physical Enter positions |\n\nEvery physical code uses brackets in strings, including names shared with logical keys such as `[Enter]` and `[F13]`. Supported codes are type-safe and available in autocomplete. Do not put a bracketed code in an object's `key` field; use `code`. A binding has either `key` or `code`, never both.\n\nOn a layout where the `KeyQ` position produces `a`, `A` follows that character and `[KeyQ]` follows the position. Logical ASCII letters remain layout-aware; conservative physical fallback helps with transformed output such as macOS Option keys. Exact matches take priority over weaker fallbacks among eligible registrations on the same target.\n\nCallbacks expose the same distinction in `context.parsedHotkey`: check `parsed.code !== undefined` before reading its physical identity. Use `formatForDisplay` for labels; stored physical strings retain their brackets.\n\n## Basic usage\n\n```tsx\nimport { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nfunction App() {\n  createHotkey('Mod+S', () => {\n    saveDocument()\n  }, {\n    // override the default options here\n  })\n}\n```\n\nThe callback receives the original `KeyboardEvent` as the first argument and a `HotkeyCallbackContext` as the second:\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', (event, context) => {\n  console.log(context.hotkey)       // 'Mod+S'\n  console.log(context.parsedHotkey) // { key: 'S', ctrl: false, shift: false, alt: false, meta: true, modifiers: ['Meta'] }\n})\n```\n\nYou can pass a hotkey as a string or as a `RawHotkey` object (modifier booleans optional). Use `mod` for cross-platform shortcuts (Command on Mac, Control elsewhere):\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())\ncreateHotkey({ key: 'S', mod: true }, () => save())           // Same as above\ncreateHotkey({ key: 'Escape' }, () => closeModal())\ncreateHotkey({ code: 'NumpadAdd', mod: true }, () => zoomIn())\ncreateHotkey({ key: 'S', ctrl: true, shift: true }, () => saveAs())\ncreateHotkey({ key: 'S', mod: true, shift: true }, () => saveAs())\n```\n\n## Reactive options\n\nSolid primitives accept property getters and accessor functions for reactive options. With an accessor, the hotkey updates automatically when dependencies change:\n\n```tsx\nfunction Modal(props) {\n  createHotkey('Escape', () => props.onClose(), () => ({\n    enabled: props.isOpen,\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <Show when={props.isOpen}>\n      <div class=\"modal\">...</div>\n    </Show>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nFor scoped targets, use an accessor so the hotkey waits for the element to be attached:\n\n```tsx\nfunction Editor() {\n  const [editorRef, setEditorRef] = createSignal<HTMLDivElement | null>(null)\n\n  createHotkey('Mod+S', save, () => ({ target: editorRef() }))\n\n  return <div ref={setEditorRef}>...</div>\n}\n```\n\n### Changing a binding\n\nPass a new logical or physical binding through your framework's normal state mechanism. A recorder result such as `Alt+[KeyS]` can be passed directly to the same registration API. Keep an initial binding in application state if you want a reset button; the library does not need a separate preferences store.\n\n## Property getters\n\nProperty getters and functions returning an options object are both supported. Read reactive state inside the getter. A plain value such as `{ enabled: currentValue }` captures the value when that object is created.\n\nThe adapter reads option properties inside its reactive computation and updates registrations automatically. Callbacks such as `onRecord` and `onCancel` remain functions; the adapter does not call them to resolve options. Tracking is shallow; callback bodies and nested objects are not evaluated to discover dependencies. Keep getters free of side effects. Ordinary option changes preserve registration identity. Changing the target moves the registration to that target.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createHotkey } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nexport function SaveShortcut(props: { enabled: boolean; onSave: () => void }) {\n\tcreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => props.onSave(), {\n\t\tget enabled() {\n\t\t\treturn props.enabled\n\t\t},\n\t})\n\treturn null\n}\n```\n\nYou can also pass `() => ({ enabled: props.enabled })`. Both forms work for common options, sequences, and recorders. Property getters also work in per-definition options. `HotkeysProvider` follows replacement `defaultOptions` objects and getters within defaults. Per-call options override provider defaults, and per-definition options override common options.\n\n## Default options\n\nWhen you register a hotkey without passing options, or when you omit specific options, the following defaults apply:\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', callback, {\n  enabled: true,\n  preventDefault: true,\n  stopPropagation: true,\n  eventType: 'keydown',\n  requireReset: false,\n  ignoreInputs: undefined, // smart default: false for Mod+S, true for single keys\n  target: document,\n  platform: undefined, // auto-detected\n  conflictBehavior: 'warn',\n})\n```\n\n### Why these defaults?\n\nMost hotkey registrations are meant to override default browser behavior, such as using `Mod+S` to save a document instead of showing the browser's \"Save Page\" dialog. So `preventDefault` and `stopPropagation` are `true` by default, and you opt out per hotkey when you actually want the browser behavior.\n\n#### Smart input handling: `ignoreInputs`\n\nBy default, hotkeys with `Ctrl`/`Meta` modifiers (like `Mod+S`) and the `Escape` key fire even when focus is inside input elements (such as text fields or text areas), and when focused on button-type inputs (`type=\"button\"`, `\"submit\"`, or `\"reset\"`). Shortcuts like save or close keep working wherever the user is focused. Single-key shortcuts, or those using only `Shift`/`Alt`, are ignored within non-button inputs so they don't interfere with normal typing.\n\n#### Hotkey conflicts: `conflictBehavior`\n\nWhen you attempt to register a hotkey that is already registered (possibly in another part of your app), the library logs a warning by default using the `conflictBehavior: 'warn'` setting. That helps you catch accidental duplicate bindings during development, before they reach production.\n\n### Global defaults via provider\n\nYou can change the default options for all `createHotkey` calls in your app by wrapping your component tree with `HotkeysProvider`. Per-primitive options override the provider defaults.\n\n```tsx\nimport { HotkeysProvider } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\n<HotkeysProvider\n  defaultOptions={{\n    hotkey: { preventDefault: false, ignoreInputs: false },\n  }}\n>\n  <App />\n</HotkeysProvider>\n```\n\n## Hotkey options\n\n### `enabled`\n\nControls whether the hotkey is active. Defaults to `true`. Use an accessor for reactive control.\n\nDisabled hotkeys remain registered in the manager and stay visible in devtools; only execution is suppressed.\n\n```tsx\nconst [isEditing, setIsEditing] = createSignal(false)\n\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), () => ({ enabled: isEditing() }))\n```\n\n### `preventDefault`\n\nAutomatically calls `event.preventDefault()` when the hotkey fires. Defaults to `true`.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { preventDefault: false })\n```\n\n### `stopPropagation`\n\nCalls `event.stopPropagation()` when the hotkey fires. Defaults to `true`.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Escape', () => closeModal())\ncreateHotkey('Escape', () => closeModal(), { stopPropagation: false })\n```\n\n### `eventType`\n\nWhether to listen on `keydown` (default) or `keyup`.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Shift', () => deactivateMode(), { eventType: 'keyup' })\n```\n\n### `requireReset`\n\nWhen `true`, the hotkey fires only once per key press. The key must be released and pressed again to fire again. Defaults to `false`.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel(), { requireReset: true })\n```\n\n### `ignoreInputs`\n\nWhen `true`, the hotkey doesn't fire when the user is focused on a text input, textarea, select, or contentEditable element. When unset, a smart default applies based on the hotkey type.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('K', () => openSearch())  // Smart default: ignored in inputs\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save())   // Smart default: fires in inputs\ncreateHotkey('Enter', () => submit(), { ignoreInputs: false })\n```\n\n### `target`\n\nThe DOM element to attach the event listener to. Defaults to `document`. Can be a DOM element, `document`, `window`, or from an accessor for reactive targets.\n\n```tsx\nconst [panelRef, setPanelRef] = createSignal<HTMLDivElement | null>(null)\n\ncreateHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel(), () => ({ target: panelRef() }))\n\nreturn <div ref={setPanelRef} tabIndex={0}>...</div>\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> When using an accessor for the target, the primitive waits for the element to be available before registering. Ensure the element is focusable (has `tabIndex`) so it can receive keyboard events.\n\n### `conflictBehavior`\n\nControls what happens when you register a hotkey that's already registered. Options: `'warn'`, `'error'`, `'replace'`, `'allow'`.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { conflictBehavior: 'replace' })\n```\n\n### `platform`\n\nOverride the auto-detected platform.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), { platform: 'mac' })\n```\n\n## Automatic dependency tracking\n\nSolid's fine-grained reactivity means `createHotkey` automatically tracks reactive dependencies. The callback always has access to the latest signal values:\n\n```tsx\nfunction Counter() {\n  const [count, setCount] = createSignal(0)\n\n  createHotkey('Mod+Shift+C', () => {\n    console.log('Current count:', count())\n  })\n\n  return <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count()}</button>\n}\n```\n\n## Automatic cleanup\n\nThe primitive automatically unregisters the hotkey when the component unmounts (when the owning reactive scope is disposed):\n\n```tsx\nfunction TemporaryPanel() {\n  createHotkey('Escape', () => closePanel())\n  return <div>Panel content</div>\n}\n```\n\n## Registering multiple hotkeys\n\nWhen you need to register several hotkeys at once, or a dynamic, variable-length list, use the `createHotkeys` (plural) primitive:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createHotkeys } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nfunction Editor() {\n  createHotkeys([\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo() },\n    { hotkey: 'Escape', callback: () => close() },\n  ])\n}\n```\n\n### Common options with per-hotkey overrides\n\nPass shared options as the second argument. Per-definition options override the common ones:\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkeys(\n  [\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+S', callback: () => save() },\n    { hotkey: 'Mod+Z', callback: () => undo(), options: { enabled: false } },\n  ],\n  { preventDefault: true },\n)\n```\n\n### Dynamic hotkey lists\n\nPass an accessor for reactive arrays:\n\n```tsx\nfunction MenuShortcuts(props) {\n  createHotkeys(\n    () => props.items.map((item) => ({\n      hotkey: item.shortcut,\n      callback: item.action,\n      options: { enabled: item.enabled },\n    })),\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe primitive tracks dependencies automatically and diffs registrations when the array changes.\n\n## Metadata (name, description, and group)\n\nEvery hotkey registration can carry a `meta` object with a `name`, `description`, and `group`. Metadata never affects hotkey behavior, but it flows through to registrations and devtools, so you can build shortcut palettes and help screens from it.\n\n```tsx\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document' },\n})\n```\n\nThe `meta` option is typed as `HotkeyMeta`, which ships with `name`, `description`, and `group` fields. You can extend it with additional properties using TypeScript declaration merging:\n\n```tsx\ndeclare module '@tanstack/hotkeys' {\n  interface HotkeyMeta {\n    icon?: string\n  }\n}\n\ncreateHotkey('Mod+S', () => save(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Save', description: 'Save the document', icon: 'floppy', group: 'File' },\n})\n```\n\nGroup is descriptive metadata, not an execution scope. A shortcuts panel can group live registration views directly. Disabled registrations remain listed; unmounted registrations disappear.\n\n## Introspecting registrations\n\nUse the `createHotkeyRegistrations` primitive to get a live view of all hotkey and sequence registrations. This is useful for building shortcut palettes, help dialogs, or devtools.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createHotkeyRegistrations, formatForDisplay } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nfunction ShortcutPalette() {\n  const registrations = createHotkeyRegistrations()\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h2>Keyboard Shortcuts</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <For each={registrations().hotkeys}>\n          {(reg) => (\n            <li>\n              <kbd>{formatForDisplay(reg.hotkey)}</kbd>\n              {reg.options.meta?.name && <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>}\n              {reg.options.meta?.description && <p>{reg.options.meta.description}</p>}\n            </li>\n          )}\n        </For>\n      </ul>\n      <Show when={registrations().sequences.length > 0}>\n        <h2>Sequences</h2>\n        <ul>\n          <For each={registrations().sequences}>\n            {(reg) => (\n              <li>\n                <kbd>{reg.sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')}</kbd>\n                {reg.options.meta?.name && <span> — {reg.options.meta.name}</span>}\n              </li>\n            )}\n          </For>\n        </ul>\n      </Show>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe returned accessor yields an object with a `hotkeys` array and a `sequences` array. Each hotkey entry carries the hotkey string, its options (including `meta`), and its enabled state; sequence entries have the same structure.\n\n## The hotkey manager\n\nUnder the hood, `createHotkey` uses the singleton `HotkeyManager`. You can also access the manager directly if needed:\n\n```tsx\nimport { getHotkeyManager } from '@tanstack/solid-hotkeys'\n\nconst manager = getHotkeyManager()\nmanager.isRegistered('Mod+S')\nmanager.getRegistrationCount()\n```\n"}
